/**
 * The door rules arm's built-in corpus (`--door-rules builtin`, `door.ts`):
 * the authoring rules the hosted product's MCP build door hands its client,
 * ported as data so a door-mode round can measure what each rule is worth by
 * running with and without it. Data only: no hosted code is ported, and
 * nothing here is read by any product package.
 *
 * ## Where the text comes from
 *
 * Rendered, not retyped, from the `objectstack-ai/cloud` repository at
 * `c9b6664d9ac8cdc16df61a40febe3b5c71f41f11`, by
 * `authoringCorporaAt(authoringTierFor('mcp', undefined, undefined, { status: 'none' }), undefined, 'mcp')`:
 * the corpora the MCP build door's `get_authoring_rules` reads, at the tier
 * its tools judge by, with each rule's MCP-door wording. Sources, all under
 * `packages/service-ai-studio/src/`:
 *
 *  - `tools/get-authoring-rules.tool.ts` (`authoringCorporaAt`)
 *  - `authoring-whitelist.ts` (`authoringTierFor`)
 *  - `skills/authoring-rule.ts` (`corpusAt`, `ruleOffered`)
 *  - `skills/authoring-source.ts` (the restriction-profile reading, `none`)
 *  - `skills/metadata-authoring-skill.ts` (the `metadata_authoring` corpus)
 *  - `skills/solution-design-skill.ts` (the `solution_design` corpus)
 *  - `skills/published-skills.ts`, `skills/published-refs.ts` (the sections a
 *    rule ends with, read from this repository's published `skills/` catalog,
 *    built at the commit this module was written on)
 *
 * Each rule's text is verbatim as that door serves it, including the
 * published sections it ends with (each headed `[published · SKILL/FILE §
 * HEADING]`) and the tracker and ADR citations the hosted text carries. Each
 * corpus's preamble is a rule of its own (`metadata-authoring-preamble`,
 * `solution-design-preamble`), first in its corpus.
 *
 * ## Left out
 *
 *  - `14b-tier`: it describes the hosted runtime's timer lifecycle (a clock
 *    trigger runs inside a per-environment kernel the hosting builds on
 *    demand), which no open runtime has.
 *  - Not offered at that tier, so not served by that door: `flow-node-types`,
 *    `13`, `14b`, `14c` (`metadata_authoring`) and `process-approval-flow`
 *    (`solution_design`).
 *
 * ## The named sets (`--door-rules-omit`)
 *
 *  - `metadata-authoring`, `solution-design`: each corpus, preamble included.
 *  - `inline`: what the hosted door hands its client up front (both preambles
 *    and every `inline` rule); `ondemand`: what it serves only when the
 *    client asks for the rule by id. `--door-rules-omit ondemand` is the
 *    closest a file of texts comes to the hosted delivery.
 *  - `names-build-door-tools`: the rules whose own text (before the first
 *    published section) names a tool the hosted build door registers
 *    (`PLATFORM_TOOLS_BY_PACKAGE['service-ai-studio']` in `@objectstack/spec`).
 *    The open MCP server serves a few of those names too (`list_objects`,
 *    `describe_object`, `validate_expression`); most it does not
 *    (`apply_blueprint`, `apply_edit`, `verify_build`, `create_metadata`,
 *    `get_authoring_rules`, …), so this set is the arm without the rules that
 *    send the client to a tool it may not hold.
 *
 * ⛔ Never edit a text by hand: a port that drifts from the hosted text
 * measures a corpus nobody serves. Re-render it from a named cloud revision.
 */

